# Revised Rust Program

**Tool feedback applied:**
- *Deadlock risk (R3/R4):* the original design risked a role blocking on a channel `recv` while holding the shared mutex, which could starve the other role. Fixed by scoping every lock acquisition in a block that releases the guard **before** any channel operation.
- *Rendezvous guarantee (R2):* the exchange is structured as a two-phase meeting — `s` sends the value on `ch1`, then blocks on `ch2` until `r` acknowledges, so both roles must meet for the value to complete its journey.
- *Termination (R4):* since no thread ever waits on a channel while holding the lock, every interleaving of the lock acquisitions and channel operations terminates; `main` joins both roles before printing.
- *Exact output (R5):* only `main` prints, exactly one line, after both roles finish.

```rust
use std::sync::{mpsc, Arc, Mutex};

// Entities:
//   Roles:            s (sender), r (receiver)
//   Shared resources: ch1 (channel), ch2 (channel), shared lock (Mutex)
//
// Protocol:
//   1. s briefly takes the shared lock, then releases it.
//   2. s sends the value on ch1; r receives it (the two roles meet on ch1).
//   3. r briefly takes the shared lock, then releases it.
//   4. r acknowledges on ch2; s receives the ack (the two roles meet on ch2).
//
// R3 is satisfied: neither role ever blocks on a channel recv/send while
// holding the lock, because each lock guard is dropped (end of block)
// before the next channel operation. Hence no deadlock is possible and
// every interleaving terminates (R4).

fn main() {
    // Shared lock used occasionally by both roles.
    let lock: Arc<Mutex<()>> = Arc::new(Mutex::new(()));

    // ch1: s -> r (value exchange, both roles must meet).
    // ch2: r -> s (acknowledgement, completing the rendezvous).
    let (ch1_tx, ch1_rx) = mpsc::channel::<i32>();
    let (ch2_tx, ch2_rx) = mpsc::channel::<i32>();

    // Role s: sender.
    let s_lock = Arc::clone(&lock);
    let s = std::thread::spawn(move || {
        // Occasionally use the shared lock — but release it before
        // touching the channel (R3).
        {
            let _guard = s_lock.lock().unwrap();
            // ... critical section for s ...
        } // lock released here

        // Send the value on ch1.
        ch1_tx.send(1).unwrap();

        // Wait for r's acknowledgement on ch2 (not holding the lock).
        let ack = ch2_rx.recv().unwrap();
        ack
    });

    // Role r: receiver.
    let r_lock = Arc::clone(&lock);
    let r = std::thread::spawn(move || {
        // Meet s on ch1 and take the value.
        let value = ch1_rx.recv().unwrap();

        // Occasionally use the shared lock — released before sending
        // the ack on ch2 (R3).
        {
            let _guard = r_lock.lock().unwrap();
            // ... critical section for r ...
        } // lock released here

        // Acknowledge on ch2 so s can finish.
        ch2_tx.send(value).unwrap();
        value
    });

    // Both roles run concurrently; join guarantees both are finished (R4).
    let s_result = s.join().unwrap();
    let r_result = r.join().unwrap();

    // The exchanged value, verified to have made the round trip
    // through ch1 and ch2.
    assert_eq!(s_result, 1);
    assert_eq!(r_result, 1);
    let done = s_result;

    // Exactly one line of output (R5).
    println!("DONE done={}", done);
}
```

**Why every schedule terminates (R4):** `s` holds the lock only inside a scoped block and drops it before sending on `ch1`; `r` holds the lock only after receiving from `ch1` and drops it before sending on `ch2`. Therefore no thread can be blocked on a channel while the other waits for the lock, so the lock always becomes available, both channel meetings complete, and both threads finish in any interleaving.

## Defect review

The naive design has each worker grab its locks in the order listed in R3: t1 takes `a` then `b`, t2 takes `b` then `c`, t3 takes `c` then `a`. That creates a **circular wait** (a → b → c → a), so an interleaving where each worker holds its first lock and blocks on its second violates R5, R8, and R9.

**Repair:** impose a global lock-acquisition order (`a` < `b` < `c`) and have every worker acquire in that order. t1 takes `a`,`b`; t2 takes `b`,`c`; t3 — which needs `c` and `a` — takes `a` **before** `c`. The cycle is broken: any worker that holds a lock always holds the lower-ordered one, so at least one worker can always acquire both of its locks and complete. R4 still holds (both guards are alive during the critical work), and R6 holds because Rust's RAII guards release on scope exit, before the thread closure returns.

## Program

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    // Shared locks a, b, c (R2: each held by at most one worker at a time).
    let a = Arc::new(Mutex::new(()));
    let b = Arc::new(Mutex::new(()));
    let c = Arc::new(Mutex::new(()));

    // Global acquisition order: a < b < c. This breaks the circular wait,
    // so no schedule can deadlock (R5, R8, R9).

    // t1 needs a and b: acquires a, then b.
    let (la, lb) = (Arc::clone(&a), Arc::clone(&b));
    let t1 = thread::spawn(move || {
        let _g1 = la.lock().unwrap(); // a
        let _g2 = lb.lock().unwrap(); // b
        // Critical work with both locks held (R4).
        // Guards drop here, releasing both locks before the thread ends (R6).
    });

    // t2 needs b and c: acquires b, then c.
    let (lb, lc) = (Arc::clone(&b), Arc::clone(&c));
    let t2 = thread::spawn(move || {
        let _g1 = lb.lock().unwrap(); // b
        let _g2 = lc.lock().unwrap(); // c
        // Critical work with both locks held (R4).
    });

    // t3 needs c and a: acquires a, then c (respects global order a < c).
    let (la, lc) = (Arc::clone(&a), Arc::clone(&c));
    let t3 = thread::spawn(move || {
        let _g1 = la.lock().unwrap(); // a
        let _g2 = lc.lock().unwrap(); // c
        // Critical work with both locks held (R4).
    });

    // Main starts all three workers and waits for all of them (R1, R7).
    t1.join().unwrap();
    t2.join().unwrap();
    t3.join().unwrap();

    // Exactly one line of output, then exit (R10).
    println!("DONE done=1");
}
```

## Why every schedule terminates

With the ordering `a < b < c`, suppose the workers run in any interleaving and all become blocked. Each blocked worker holds its lower-ordered lock and waits on a higher-ordered one. Consider the worker holding `a` (at least one must, or `a` is free and its seeker would have taken it). That worker waits only on `b` or `c` — never on `a` — so the wait graph is acyclic; some worker can always finish, release its locks, and progress cascades. Hence no deadlock under any interleaving, and `main` joins all three threads and prints the required line.

